Title: EXTI-HAL (i.e. not GPIO-HAL using EXTI)

Body:
Example Code with error

void myEXTIinit(void)
{
  EXTI_ConfigTypeDef myExtiConfig;

  HAL_EXTI_RegisterCallback(&hexti13, HAL_EXTI_COMMON_CB_ID , 
                              &myEXTI13IRQHandler);

  myExtiConfig.Line = EXTI_LINE_13;
  myExtiConfig.Mode = EXTI_MODE_INTERRUPT;
  myExtiConfig.Trigger = EXTI_TRIGGER_RISING;
  HAL_EXTI_SetConfigLine(&hexti13, &myExtiConfig);
}

HAL_EXTI_SetConfigLine() produces a Hard-Fault

### Reason:

HAL_StatusTypeDef HAL_EXTI_SetConfigLine(EXTI_HandleTypeDef *hexti, EXTI_ConfigTypeDef *pExtiConfig)
{
  uint32_t regval; // temporary variable to calculate peripheral address is a integer type not a pointer

// ...
// EXTI_MODE_INTERRUPT = 1
regval = (uint32_t)EXTI_BASE;
regval += pExtiConfig->Mode; // >> leads to odd number
*(__IO uint32_t *) regval |= pExtiConfig->Line; // illegal access for peripherals and wrong address for mode

//...
}

- Same problem for trigger rising or falling edge -> additional wrong base address for RTSR, FTSR

- Register EXTI-IMR is cleared but not set again by  HAL_EXTI_SetConfigLine() >> no interrupt

In the meanwhile, please find attached this patch [STM32F7xx_HAL_Driver.zip](https://github.com/STMicroelectronics/STM32CubeF7/files/3570207/STM32F7xx_HAL_Driver.zip) implementing the fix. Once unzipped, please replace your current HAL EXTI .h and .c files with the ones within.

We hope this helps you solve the problem. Do not hesitate in case something still does not work properly.

With regards,

